## Preamble

The CIEL Research Non-Commercial License v2.0 is a source-available research license intended to permit meaningful inspection, execution, modification, reproducibility testing, benchmarking, scientific criticism, security review, and non-commercial research while preserving the Licensor’s rights concerning commercial exploitation, production deployment, hosted services, model development, model extraction, and machine-learning training.

This license is not an Open Source Initiative approved open-source license.

The core distinction of this license is:

> **Using the Licensed Materials as a research tool is permitted within the scope of this license. Using the Licensed Materials to train, derive, reproduce, improve, or commercially operate another model, product, or service is not.**

---

## 1. Definitions

### 1.1 Licensor

“Licensor” means Adrian Lipa / Intention Lab, or a lawful successor or assignee of the relevant rights.

### 1.2 Licensee

“You” or “Licensee” means the individual or legal entity exercising rights under this license.

### 1.3 Licensed Materials

“Licensed Materials” means the software and project materials distributed under this license, including source code, scripts, executable code, architecture files, specifications, protocols, schemas, tests, benchmark harnesses, examples, documentation, agent definitions, configuration files, prompts intentionally distributed as part of the project, and other files expressly included in the licensed repository or release.

Licensed Materials do not automatically include unrelated data, content, research results, or outputs created by a Licensee merely because those materials were processed using the Software.

### 1.4 Software

“Software” means the executable and non-executable software components of the Licensed Materials.

### 1.5 Research Use

“Research Use” means use for personal study, independent research, academic research, teaching, reproducibility, debugging, benchmarking, scientific evaluation, security analysis, interoperability research, performance analysis, or other non-commercial investigation.

### 1.6 Non-Commercial Use

“Non-Commercial Use” means use whose primary purpose is not commercial advantage, revenue generation, paid service delivery, product development, organizational production activity, customer delivery, investment diligence, procurement, resale, or monetization.

Receipt of ordinary academic salary, scholarship, public research funding, or non-commercial research grants does not by itself make an otherwise permitted activity Commercial Use.

### 1.7 Commercial Use

“Commercial Use” includes, without limitation:

a. incorporation into a commercial product or service;  
b. internal business tooling that materially supports commercial operations;  
c. paid consulting or contracting using the Licensed Materials;  
d. customer demonstrations or customer-facing evaluation;  
e. investor, acquisition, or procurement diligence;  
f. resale, relicensing, sublicensing, or monetized distribution;  
g. paid hosted access;  
h. product development intended for commercial deployment.

Commercial Use requires a separate written license from the Licensor.

### 1.8 Production Use

“Production Use” means deployment in a live operational environment relied upon for business, infrastructure, customers, users, decision-making, compliance, safety, revenue generation, or continuing organizational operations.

### 1.9 Hosted Service

“Hosted Service” means providing third parties with access to the Software or materially equivalent functionality through a network, API, website, cloud service, agent service, managed service, SaaS offering, plugin service, hosted application, or similar mechanism.

### 1.10 Reproducibility Activity

“Reproducibility Activity” means executing, measuring, profiling, testing, reproducing, challenging, falsifying, comparing, or independently validating claims concerning the Licensed Materials.

### 1.11 Permitted Automated Analysis

“Permitted Automated Analysis” means use of compilers, interpreters, profilers, debuggers, linters, static-analysis tools, test frameworks, fuzzers, symbolic tools, numerical libraries, vectorized execution systems, code-search systems, performance tools, or general-purpose computational assistants solely to inspect, execute, test, understand, debug, or evaluate the Licensed Materials within permitted Research Use.

### 1.12 AI/ML Development Use

“AI/ML Development Use” means using the Licensed Materials, their protected internal behavior, or substantial generated datasets derived from them for:

a. model training;  
b. fine-tuning;  
c. distillation;  
d. reinforcement learning;  
e. preference optimization;  
f. policy learning;  
g. alignment training;  
h. model improvement;  
i. creation of learned adapters or weights;  
j. creation of persistent embedding corpora intended to reproduce or operationalize the Licensed Materials;  
k. automated generation of training datasets for another model.

AI/ML Development Use is prohibited without separate written authorization.

### 1.13 Model Artifact

“Model Artifact” means model weights, adapters, LoRAs, learned policies, embedding databases, training datasets, distilled representations, synthetic training corpora, learned retrieval indexes, or other machine-learned artifacts materially derived from the Licensed Materials.

### 1.14 Model Extraction

“Model Extraction” means systematic use of the Software, its outputs, traces, decisions, intermediate states, or interfaces with the purpose or substantial effect of reproducing, imitating, approximating, cloning, or transferring its protected behavior into another automated system.

### 1.15 Transient Computational Representation

“Transient Computational Representation” means a temporary representation such as RAM state, cache, tensor, temporary embedding, compiler intermediate representation, execution trace, or search index created solely as part of a permitted Research Use and discarded when that activity is complete.

A Transient Computational Representation is not a prohibited Model Artifact solely because it is machine-readable.

### 1.16 Output

“Output” means information generated through execution of the Software.

The Licensor claims no ownership merely because an Output was generated using the Software.

To the extent an Output reproduces copyrightable portions of the Licensed Materials themselves, those reproduced portions remain subject to applicable rights and this license.

### 1.17 Derivative Work

“Derivative Work” means a work based upon copyrightable expression contained in the Licensed Materials, including modifications, adaptations, translations, ports, or forks.

Independent implementation of an abstract idea, algorithmic concept, mathematical principle, scientific concept, method, or functionality is governed by applicable law and is not deemed a Derivative Work merely because the underlying idea is similar.

### 1.18 Offensive Use

“Offensive Use” means use materially intended to facilitate armed attack, harmful cyber intrusion, malware deployment, unauthorized surveillance for harmful purposes, weapon guidance, target selection, coercive control, unlawful exploitation, or intentional harm to persons or civil infrastructure.

