## 2. Grant of Research Rights

Subject to compliance with this license, the Licensor grants You a worldwide, royalty-free, non-exclusive, non-transferable, non-sublicensable license to:

2.1 read and inspect the Licensed Materials;

2.2 download and copy them as reasonably necessary for permitted Research Use;

2.3 execute the Software locally;

2.4 modify the Software for private or non-commercial research;

2.5 create non-commercial research forks;

2.6 perform Reproducibility Activities;

2.7 conduct benchmarking and performance analysis;

2.8 perform permitted security analysis;

2.9 use Permitted Automated Analysis;

2.10 submit patches, issues, test results, documentation, or pull requests to the Licensor;

2.11 publish permitted research results subject to Section 9.

No rights are granted beyond those expressly stated.

---

## 3. Research Execution Safe Harbor

The following activities are expressly permitted when performed for Research Use:

3.1 executing built-in agent systems;

3.2 executing multi-agent orchestration included in the Software;

3.3 running schedulers, routers, worker systems, vector lanes, resident workers, pipelines, graph systems, or other native project mechanisms;

3.4 changing execution schedules, batching strategies, worker counts, phase relationships, vector widths, or runtime parameters;

3.5 profiling CPU, GPU, memory, latency, throughput, queue behavior, numerical error, convergence, or computational complexity;

3.6 performing scalar versus vector comparisons;

3.7 performing SIMD, BLAS, GPU, CPU, accelerator, or other hardware optimization experiments;

3.8 modifying the Software to test alternative execution strategies;

3.9 reproducing benchmark claims made by the Licensor;

3.10 attempting to disprove or falsify such benchmark claims.

These activities do not become prohibited AI/ML Development Use merely because the Software itself contains agents, automated reasoning, machine-learning components, or vectorized computation.

---

## 4. Tool Use Is Distinct from Model Development

Using the Licensed Materials as a tool during permitted Research Use does not by itself constitute AI/ML Development Use.

For clarity, You may use GREMLIN, CIEL, or related Licensed Software to:

4.1 analyze research material;

4.2 evaluate hypotheses;

4.3 generate candidate relations;

4.4 perform computational searches;

4.5 execute project-native agent orchestration;

4.6 perform mathematical or numerical experiments;

4.7 test project-native reasoning or scheduling architectures.

However, You may not use the Licensed Materials to train, teach, distill, clone, optimize, or materially improve a separate model or automated system except under separate written authorization.

---

## 5. Automated Tools and General-Purpose AI Assistants

Permitted Research Use may involve general-purpose automated tools or AI assistants provided that:

5.1 their use is directed by a human researcher;

5.2 the purpose is understanding, testing, debugging, documenting, or evaluating the Licensed Materials;

5.3 no persistent Model Artifact is created from the Licensed Materials;

5.4 the Licensed Materials are not knowingly supplied for provider-side model training or model improvement;

5.5 the activity is not Commercial Use, Production Use, or Hosted Service use;

5.6 any generated transient representations are limited to what is reasonably necessary for the research activity.

This section permits Human-Directed Transient Analysis and supersedes any interpretation that ordinary debugging, code assistance, profiling, or research analysis is automatically prohibited AI/ML Use.

---

## 6. Training and Model Development Firewall

Without separate written authorization from the Licensor, You may not use the Licensed Materials for:

6.1 training a model;

6.2 fine-tuning a model;

6.3 distilling a model;

6.4 alignment or preference optimization;

6.5 reinforcement-learning policy development;

6.6 creation of learned adapters;

6.7 generating a training corpus intended to teach another system the Licensed Materials;

6.8 persistent embedding or vector-database construction whose purpose is to operationalize or reproduce substantial protected content;

6.9 creating learned representations of protected internal architecture;

6.10 improving a commercial or production AI model through systematic exposure to the Licensed Materials.

---

## 7. Model Extraction and Behavioral Cloning

Without separate written authorization, You may not:

7.1 systematically query the Software for the purpose of reconstructing its protected behavior;

7.2 build a competing automated system from large-scale input/output pairs produced by the Software;

7.3 use execution traces, agent decisions, routing behavior, hidden states, intermediate representations, benchmark corpora, or generated candidates to train a substitute system;

7.4 perform knowledge distillation from the Software;

7.5 reproduce protected project architecture through automated behavioral extraction.

Ordinary scientific observation, manual analysis, independent reimplementation of public mathematical ideas, and permitted reproducibility research do not constitute Model Extraction merely because the researcher learns from observing the Software.

---

## 8. Forks and Modifications

You may create and maintain forks or modifications solely for permitted Non-Commercial Research Use.

Any distributed permitted fork must:

8.1 retain the copyright notice;

8.2 retain this license;

8.3 clearly identify that it has been modified;

8.4 not imply endorsement by the Licensor;

8.5 not grant downstream rights broader than those granted here with respect to the original Licensed Materials.

Private research modifications need not be published.

---

## 9. Publication and Benchmarking

You may publish:

9.1 benchmark results;

9.2 performance measurements;

9.3 scientific findings;

9.4 negative results;

9.5 criticism;

9.6 reproducibility studies;

9.7 security findings, subject to responsible disclosure where appropriate;

9.8 methodological descriptions;

9.9 charts, tables, aggregate results, and reasonable code excerpts necessary to support a research claim.

You are not required to obtain approval from the Licensor before publishing good-faith research results.

You may not falsely represent results as having been produced, endorsed, certified, or verified by the Licensor.

